.page grep 1 print lines that match a pattern

# SYNOPSIS
*grep* [_OPTION_...] _PATTERN_ [_FILE_...]
*grep* [_OPTION_...] *-e* _PATTERN_ ... [*-f* _PATFILE_] [_FILE_...]

# DESCRIPTION
`grep` reads each _FILE_ (standard input if there is none, or for the name `-`) and prints the lines that match _PATTERN_. By default the pattern is a basic regular expression (see regex(7)). With more than one file, or with `-r`, each line is preceded by the file name.

A file whose first 512 bytes contain a NUL byte is binary: instead of printing matching lines `grep` prints `Binary file NAME matches`, unless `-a` is given. A pipe is checked line by line.

# PATTERN OPTIONS
@ *-E*, *--extended-regexp*
    The pattern is an extended regular expression (`|`, `+`, `?`, `{}` and `()` are operators). `-P` is treated the same way.
@ *-F*, *--fixed-strings*
    The pattern is a plain string, or several plain strings, one to a line.
@ *-G*
    Basic regular expressions: the default.
@ *-e* _PATTERN_, *--regexp*=_PATTERN_
    Use _PATTERN_; may be repeated, and a line matching any one of them is selected. Useful when the pattern starts with a dash.
@ *-f* _FILE_, *--file*=_FILE_
    Read patterns from _FILE_, one per line.
@ *-i*, *-y*, *--ignore-case*
    Ignore case.
@ *-v*, *--invert-match*
    Select the lines that do not match.
@ *-w*, *--word-regexp*
    The match must be a whole word: not preceded or followed by a letter, digit or underscore.
@ *-x*, *--line-regexp*
    The match must be the whole line.

# OUTPUT OPTIONS
@ *-c*, *--count*
    Print only the number of selected lines for each file.
@ *-l*, *--files-with-matches*
    Print only the names of files that have a match.
@ *-L*, *--files-without-match*
    Print only the names of files that have none.
@ *-o*, *--only-matching*
    Print only the matched parts, one per line.
@ *-q*, *--quiet*, *--silent*
    Print nothing; exit 0 as soon as a line is selected.
@ *-s*, *--no-messages*
    Do not complain about files that cannot be read.
@ *-m* _NUM_, *--max-count*=_NUM_
    Stop reading a file after _NUM_ selected lines.
@ *-n*, *--line-number*
    Put the line number in front of each line.
@ *-b*, *--byte-offset*
    Put the byte offset of each line (or, with `-o`, of each match) in front of it.
@ *-H*, *--with-filename*
    Always print the file name.
@ *-h*, *--no-filename*
    Never print the file name.
@ *-Z*, *--null*
    Follow a file name by a NUL byte instead of the usual colon or newline.
@ *--color*[=_WHEN_]
    Mark the matches in colour (bold red; file names magenta, line numbers green). _WHEN_ is `always`, `auto` (only on a terminal) or `never`. Without a value it means `always`. The profile of every login shell defines `alias grep='grep --color=auto'`.

# CONTEXT
@ *-A* _N_, *--after-context*=_N_
    Print _N_ lines after each match.
@ *-B* _N_, *--before-context*=_N_
    Print _N_ lines before each match.
@ *-C* _N_, *--context*=_N_, *-*_N_
    Print _N_ lines on both sides. Groups that are not adjacent are separated by a line of `--`. Context lines are marked with `-` instead of `:` after the file name and line number.

# FILE SELECTION
@ *-r*, *--recursive*
    Read all files under each directory (the current directory if none is named). Symbolic links met while descending are not followed.
@ *-R*, *--dereference-recursive*
    The same, following symbolic links.
@ *--include*=_GLOB_
    Search only files whose base name matches _GLOB_ (`*` and `?` only).
@ *--exclude*=_GLOB_
    Skip files whose base name matches _GLOB_.
@ *--exclude-dir*=_GLOB_
    Skip directories whose base name matches _GLOB_.
@ *-a*, *--text*
    Treat binary files as text.
@ *-I*
    Accepted for compatibility.

# EXIT STATUS
0 when a line was selected, 1 when none was, 2 when there was an error (unless `-q` found a match).

# EXAMPLES
    grep reimu /Hakurei/etc/passwd
    grep -rn "TODO" /HumanVillage/reimu
    ps | grep -v grep | grep gapsh
    grep -c . notes.txt
    grep -E 'cat|dog' pets.txt
    dmesg | grep -i -A2 usb

# SEE ALSO
egrep(1), fgrep(1), sed(1), find(1), regex(7)
